## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to architect a multi-tenant data pipeline with configurable reliability guarantees, encompassing tenant isolation, fault tolerance, performance optimization, and comprehensive observability—all critical for enterprise-grade streaming solutions.

## Key Concepts

- **Tenant Partitioning**: Dedicated Kinesis streams per tenant with isolated processing contexts
- **Processing Guarantees**: Exactly-once semantics via DynamoDB deduplication vs. at-least-once with retry mechanisms
- **Backpressure Management**: Dynamic shard autoscaling and Lambda concurrency controls based on SLA requirements
- **Observability**: OpenTelemetry integration for end-to-end monitoring and performance metrics
- **Audit & Compliance**: S3 metadata tracking and Glue catalog for data lineage and governance
- **Cost Optimization**: Per-tenant resource allocation and usage-based cost attribution

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: tenant-aware deduplication and persistence
async function persistTelemetryEvent(ctx, event) {
  const deduplicationKey = `${ctx.tenantId}:${event.eventId}`;

  // Check for existing event to ensure exactly-once semantics
  const existingEvent = await checkDeduplication(deduplicationKey);
  if (!existingEvent) {
    await writeToProcessedSink(ctx.tenantId, event);
    await recordDeduplication(deduplicationKey);
  }

  // Update tenant-specific metrics for cost accounting
  await updateTenantMetrics(ctx.tenantId, event.size);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical end-to-end streaming with enrichment, idempotency, error handling, and observability in a beginner-friendly AWS DVA context.

## Key Concepts
- Kinesis Data Streams, Lambda enrichment, DynamoDB cache
- REST API rate-limiting handling and retries
- Idempotent S3 writes with Parquet partitioning
- DLQ, tracing (X-Ray), and LocalStack testing

## Code Example
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const dynamo = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();
const s3 = new AWS.S3();

async function enrich(event) {
  const id = event.deviceId + ':' + event.ts;
  let meta = await dynamo.get({TableName:'DeviceMeta', Key:{id}}).promise().then(r=>r.Item?.meta);
  if (!meta) {
    const resp = await fetchMetaFromApi(event.deviceId);
    meta = resp.meta;
    await dynamo.put({TableName:'DeviceMeta', Item:{id, meta}}).promise();
  }
  event.meta = meta;
  return event;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle DynamoDB cold starts or cache stampedes in this flow?
- How would you instrument LocalStack tests to validate retry/backoff and DLQ routing?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical knowledge of runtime schema validation, per-tenant partitioning, and end-to-end observability in a beginner-friendly context. It focuses on safe schema evolution, reliable error handling, and basic testing with LocalStack.

## Key Concepts
- AWS Glue Schema Registry for runtime JSON schema management
- Lambda-based validation with quality scoring logic
- S3 Parquet partitioning strategy by tenant and date
- DLQ handling for invalid records using SQS
- X-Ray tracing across producer, validation, and write stages
- LocalStack testing strategy for pipeline validation

## Code Example
```javascript
const { GlueSchemaRegistryClient, RegisterSchemaCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-glue-schema-registry");
const AWSXRay = require("aws-xray-sdk");

AWSXRay.enableAutomaticSampling();
AWSXRay.captureHTTPsGlobal(require("http"));

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const segment = AWSXRay.getSegment();
  const subsegment = segment.addNewSubsegment("validation");

  try {
    const qualityScore = computeQualityScore(event);

    if (qualityScore < 0.5 || !validateSchema(event)) {
      await sendToDLQ(event, qualityScore);
      return { statusCode: 400, body: "Invalid record" };
    }

    await writeToS3Parquet(event, qualityScore);
    return { statusCode: 200, body: "Success" };
  } finally {
    subsegment.close();
  }
};

function computeQualityScore(record) {
  const requiredFields = ["deviceId", "tenantId", "timestamp", "payload"];
  const presentFields = requiredFields.filter(f => record[f] !== undefined);
  return presentFields.length / requiredFields.length;
}
```

